  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "Every division problem can be written as a fraction and vice-versa. In this lesson, we'll extend this idea to division problems involving negative numbers.\n\nTo begin, let's find a fraction that's equivalent to the following division problem:\n\n$$\n(-5) \\div 9\n$$\n\nNotice that we are dividing a negative number ${(\\color{red}{-5}})$ by a positive one $({\\color{blue}{9}}).$ So the result will be negative:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14152110/assets/eb07d0730c5d11a167ac5a11813a404d464e1c0b64a87e0858c1929ce67deaaa.svg)\n\nTo write down the fraction that goes in the box, recall that \n\n$$\n5 \\div 9 = \\dfrac{5}{9}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}(-5)} \\div {\\color{blue}9} = {\\color{red}-\\dfrac{5}{9}}.\n$$",
